\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 1999, we created a program, Mega2 [], to aid us in our linkage studies.
 We found it necessary to analyze the data with several different programs;
 it was often difficult to get data into the format required by each different
 linkage computer program.
 Not only must the data be converted to the proper format, but also the
 loci must be reordered into their proper order.
 Writing custom reformatting scripts can be error-prone and time-consuming.
 We created the Mega2 program to facilitate these conversions; the initial
 Mega2 could read input data in LINKAGE format or Mega2 table format and
 convert to a few standard package like Loki [], Mendel [], and Merlin [].
 In addition, Mega2 allowed trait locus penetrance data, allele frequencies
 data, etc.
 to be added and used by the linkage programs.
 Necessary steps, like calculating the allele frequencies if necessary and
 not provided, or cleaning the data for HWE consistency can be requested.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As time went on, other linkage analysis programs where added to the set
 of programs that Mega2 could massage data for.
 Now, there are over 40 programs supported among them: SOLAR, PLINK, SimWalk2,
 Cranefoot, IQLS, FBAT, MORGAN, BEAGLE, Eigenstrat, Structure, and PLINK/SEQ.
 In addition, several new inputs are supported such as PLINK [], VCF/BCF
 [], and IMPUTE/BGEN [] formats are processed.
 The new data input formats were needed to represent additional data as
 well as more data than were available just a few years earlier.
 To cope with the additional data, both contents and processing time, Mega2
 was enhanced to store genotype data in compressed format (as is commonly
 done) and the results of data cleaning and allele frequency calculation
 was stored in a canonical format so that different linkage analysis programs
 can use these data without recalculating them for each use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The canonical representation for the pedigree, genotype and phenotype data
 is stored in a SQLite3 database[].
 Processing libraries for SQLite3 databases are available in several popular
 computer languages.
 In our case, many programs for linkage analysis are available in R [] but
 they require the genetic data to be converted to R data frame format.
 We created an R package, Mega2R [], available via CRAN [], which can convert
 the canonical data stored in SQLite3 into R data frames.
 The package also provides data to decompress genotype data and iterate
 over ranges of markers and genes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unfortunately, with all the GWAS data generated in a study especially if
 imputation [] is performed, Mega2 is faced again with too much genotype
 data for it to process.
 But fortunately, the new genotype data is generated separately for each
 chromosome; in addition, most analyses can only be done one chromosome
 at a time.
 Mega2 can take advantage of this orthogonality and generate and subsequently
 process a distinct SQLite3 database for each chromosome.
 And it can process each chromosome's data at the same time on a sufficiently
 powerful multiprocessor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Overview
\end_layout

