\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Working Directory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2 can process a set of input files that contains a genotype file
 with multiple chromosomes as it always has.
 The full explanation is in the Mega2 manual.
 But the same Mega2, given select command line flags, can process files
 of chromosomes, for an arbitrary list of chromosomes and on multiple machines.
 The first thing to note is that Mega2 must be careful about which directories
 to store data in.
 For example, suppose we were computing Merlin and Mendel results on the
 same set of chromosomes at the same time (different machines).
 It would be very easy to have the MEGA2.LOG (and related files) be written
 to the same directory and have one an unusable mess.
 If the MEGA2.LOG (etc) file was stored in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

summary
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 directory (named by the date: year-month-date-hour-minute), it is till
 possible that two different computers might start a task at exactly the
 same time.
 Most of the data files for Merlin and Mendel are named differently and
 would likely not be blended; this can not be guaranteed in general.
 So the first necessary solution step is to generate a working directory
 that at least had the chromosome number at part of its name.
 It would also be useful to add a component with the name of the analysis
 that could also serve as the name of the processor job.
 Thus we can easily separate the Mendel job from the Merlin job.
 Finally, Mega2 has always supported an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Output_Directory
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 field to indicate where relative to the current directory the generated
 results should be stored.
 Often this value is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which means that the results should be in the current directory.
 But this field could be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Mendel
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the Mendel jobs and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Merlin
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the Merlin jobs.
 The lesson from all this is the data are stored in the the directory constructe
d below relative to the current directory:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Job_Name/Output_Directory/chrxx
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Output_Directory can be empty.
 But Job_Name and the chromosome name (chrxx) must be present.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
MEGA2.BATCH file
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It would be rather inconvenient to enter the same (or similar) data 23 times
 to create processing jobs for all the chromosomes.
 We will take advantage of the MEGA2.BATCH file to simplify and standardize
 multi chromosome operation.
 Firstly, you should find a MEGA2.BATCH file that you have used before for
 the kind of analysis you plan to do.
 Alternatively, you can manually start an interactive Mega2 run which will
 as a side affect create a MEGA2.BATCH file.
 (Note: you might process chromosome 21 or 22 for this task; they tend to
 have the fewest markers.) Now when you are supplying chromosome arguments
 (will show you in just a bit) you can follow the MEGA2.BATCH file on the
 command line with more arguments, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
mega2 <options> <chromosome options> MEGA2.BATCH arg1 arg2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the MEGA2.BATCH file is processed each line is scanned for a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

$
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

%
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 followed by a digit (e.g.
 $1, $2, %2); if one is found, the two characters are replaced by the indicated
 argument following MEGA2.BATCH on the command line.
 Suppose the MEGA2.BATCH has a line that reads:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Output_Path=%2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This would be rendered in the file as
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Output_Path=arg2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

arg2
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 being the corresponding text from the command line.
 Thus we might be able to use the exact same BATCH file for both Mendel
 and Merlin and have the output go to different directories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course, this example is not the real reason for this mechanism; we want
 to indicate what chromosome we are processing and possibly where to find
 the SQLite3 database that corresponds to that chromosome.
 Our convention is that argument 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 contains the chromosome that this invocation of Mega2 is processing.
 Thus to set the chromosome for processing we would use the line
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Chromosome_Single=$0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And to chose the database that contains the chromosome, we would use
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
DBfile_name=<mydir>/%1/db%0.db
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The %1 fetches the first argument after the BATCH argument from the
 command line, allowing me to organize all the database files into a single
 directory.
 I tend to use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

db
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the directory name or possibly 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dbx
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: %0/$0 always expands to two characters (for chromosomes 1 to 22).
 In particular, if the chromosome is 7, then %0/$0 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

07
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 – not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

7
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 If you are have a file containing sample data, it may be named by just
 the chromosome number without the leading 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 In this case, you may use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

+
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 vs 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the second digit, viz.
 %+/$+ instead of the %0/$0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
<chromosome options>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2 <chromosome options> is indicated by the keyword 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

- -chr
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (or -c) followed by a list or range of numbers, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
–chr 1,2,3-5
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-c 1-22
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

chr
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 begins with two dashes.
 The first line indicates chromosome 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; the second line indicates
 all the autosomes.
 When only one chromosome is specified, Mega2 processes the analysis requested
 for that chromosome.
 When multiple chromosomes are specified, a separate Mega2 program is created
 and invoked for each chromosome and the chromosomes are processed one after
 another.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
<multiprocessor options>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As hinted above, we will take a very course approach to parallelism with
 Mega2.
 Our goal will be to process all the chromosomes at the same time.
 Of course this will mean that some some chromosomes will have their results
 computed before others.
 Another approach, which we leave for later, is to process a marker at a
 time; the locking is more complicated.
 The important point to note is that you will be submitting multiple independent
 tasks, twenty two or so each using one processor.
 You will not need more than one processor per tasks.
 Mega2 will create one background task for each chromosome.
 The command line will start with srun/qbatch depending on batching system
 followed by options, indicated below, that you select for the batch system,
 then finally for an invocation of Mega2 and the arguments you provided
 to the Mega2 command you provided.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option is indicated by
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

--queue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Name or
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-q Name
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The name is used by the underlying multiprocessor scheduler.
 We have experimented with both the Sun Grid Engine [] and Slurm [].
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Name
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 will be used for the name of the job that is created.
 This option will invoke a menu to collect parameters for either engine
 first asking for a choice of engine.
 In both cases, you will be asked for a memory limit for each created task.
 The task will be terminated when the memory limit is hit; you will have
 to experiment with the value.
 Also charging is often dependent on the amount of memory used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are many other options available for each job system.
 Describing them is beyond the scope of this document.
 Some limit the cpu time, others let you set the name of the standard out
 and standard error files.
 But the menus will allow you to select a list of desired options.
 One detail to note is that Mega2 provides a few options and their values
 as a default for each engine.
 If you provide any options besides the memory limit, the default option
 values will not be provided and you will have to supply what the default
 values should be, if you choose to use the defaults at all.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Sun Grid Engine
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
default option
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-o o<process><chr> -o e<process><chr>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Slurm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
default option
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-cwd
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
<program option>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You will initially invoke a single Mega2 program which will launch a cascade
 of Mega2 programs.
 The subsequent invocations will have the execution PATH that is established
 by Parallel Engine.
 Sometimes Mega2 is not on this later PATH.
 You may indicate a relative or absolute path to the secondary Mega2 by
 providing the option below
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
- -cmd <mega2program>
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-m <mega2program>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
<execution option>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hopefully, you will recall that as part of the preparations that Mega2 does
 for data analysis a shell script is created that will invoked the analysis
 program with the data Mega2 has prepared.
 There is a Mega2 option, –exec, that causes Mega2 to invoke the shell script
 rather than Mega2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
- -exec
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-e
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option solves two problems: The first is that is puts the directory
 components together to access the directory that contains the script and
 the working data.
 The second is that all you may select a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

–queue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 option and arguments as with Mega2; in which case all the invocations of
 the analysis for the selected chromosomes will be done in parallel.
 As you might expect, without a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

–queue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 option, analyses are performed one chromosome at a time sequentially.
 Note: We have seen cases where the parallel analysis on different chromosomes
 but done at the same time does not appear to compute the correct results;
 Be careful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
supplying a custom script
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you supply a program, with the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

–cmd
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 option, that program will be run rather than the provided analysis shell
 script.
 It will be provided with the several path components as separate arguments
 that are necessary to access the analysis directory.
 These arguments are then followed by any additional arguments provided
 on the initial Mega2 command line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
<script option>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is an advanced feature that you will probably not need or want to use,
 but I suppose it might be usefull depending on how the Multiprocessor resources
 are charged for.
 The SLURM model allows you to define a script which is invoked with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

sbatch
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The script looks like a shell script, but you may provide options and allocate
 resourses on what looks like a comment line.
 Subsequent lines in the script may be steps that also may have options
 and shares the resources of the batch.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
- - script path
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-s path
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option causes Mega2 to generate an sbatch file rather than run any
 programs.
 You are expected to add options to finish the sbatch files before you run
 it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 -q db -c 1-X -m ~/bin/Mega2 samoa/C11/BATCH db samoa/C11/all.hght freeze.5
 minDP10 "--min-af 0.05"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 -q PLINK -c 1-22 -m ~/bin/Mega2 work/PLINK work/db plink2 "--maf 0.1
 --hwe 1e-2 --geno 0.1 --mind 0.1 --linear" Mega2 -q PLINK -c 1-22 -m ~/bin/Mega2
 work/PLINK work/db plink1 "--maf 0.1 --hwe 1e-2 --geno 0.1 --mind 0.1 --assoc
 --missing-phenotype -9 --noweb"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 -n PLINK -c 1-22 -o plink2 -e plink Mega2 -q PLINK -c 1-22 -o plink1
 -e plink
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 -n PLINK -c 1-22 -m bin/pl2 -o plink2 -e plink|sort --key=12g |head
 -2000|tee plink.2.PHENO1.glm.linear Mega2 -n PLINK -c 1-22 -m bin/pl -o plink1
 -e plink|sort --key=9g |head -2000|tee plink.1.qassoc
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4/17/19 Mega2 -q work/db -c 20-22 -m ~/~/bin/Mega2 work/BATCH work/db work/1000G.
fam "--min-af 0.05" Mega2 -q work/db -c 22 -m ~/~/bin/Mega2 work/BATCH work/db
 work/1000G.fam "--min-af 0.05" 
\end_layout

